# Parental Controls: allow the service user to lock/unlock accounts and
# manage user sessions. Scoped to exact binaries; usernames are validated
# by the app ([A-Za-z0-9_.-]+, must exist in /etc/passwd).
# Install as /etc/sudoers.d/parental-controls (mode 0440) and check
# with `visudo -c`.
#
# Both /usr/sbin and /usr/bin are listed because the app invokes bare names
# (`sudo usermod`, `sudo loginctl`, `sudo passwd`) and sudo resolves them via
# secure_path (sbin first). The pairs are hardlinks to the same files, but
# sudo matches on the path string, so only the resolved path authorizes.
# `passwd` is scoped to `-S` (read-only status check) — never password changes.
#
# `systemd-run` exists for the notification path only. notify_user() runs
#   systemd-run --machine=<user>@.host --user notify-send -u critical <t> <b>
# so pre-lock warnings reach the child's own session. The machine transport
# talks to the *system* bus, which polkit refuses for the unprivileged service
# user ("Failed to connect to system scope bus via machine transport:
# Permission denied"), so the call has to arrive through sudo. Root is the only
# runas that can open it. Only notify-send is authorized as the payload: sudoers
# matches arguments as one concatenated string, so a bare `--user *` would also
# authorize any other command in a child's session (sudoers(5), "Preventing
# shell escapes").
#
# NOTE: sudoers authorizes the `systemd-run ...` command, not sudo itself, so
# this rule applies when the service reaches it through sudo — the form every
# other privileged call in the app uses.
#
# ARGUMENTS ARE REGEX-ANCHORED. sudoers(5): "If the arguments in a Cmnd begin
# with the '^' character, they will be interpreted as a regular expression and
# matched accordingly" -- the regex covers the ARGUMENTS ONLY, after the
# command path. A trailing-wildcard argument form is unsafe: sudo matches
# arguments as one concatenated string and `*` spans whitespace, so
# `usermod -U *` also authorized `usermod -U -p <hash> root` and
# `terminate-user *` authorized `terminate-user --all`. The patterns below pin
# each argv; usernames are further constrained to the app's own charset and
# `passwd -S` is pinned so no other flag can ride along. See sudo-audit.md §5.
#
# PAM SESSIONS DISABLED FOR THE SERVICE USER'S SUDO CHILDREN.
# Every sudo opens a PAM session, which pam_systemd registers with logind,
# and logind caps each user at MaxSessions (default 5) sessions of class
# user. The probe/enforcement bursts blew through that cap: logind answered
# io.systemd.Login.TooManySessions, pam_open_session failed, and sudo died
# before running even harmless read-only queries
# (`sudo: pam_open_session: Permission denied` -> the Overview's "needs
# help" lock status and the "unavailable" audit events).
#
# Grammar (sudoers(5), verified against the sudo 1.9 manual): a Default
# entry scoped with `Defaults:` lists the INVOKING users; `Defaults!` is
# the COMMAND scope — do not "correct" this line to that form. The
# `pam_session` flag (sudo >= 1.8.7) controls PAM session setup; `!`
# switches it off. The same Defaults line is repeated in the budgets
# template because sudoers merges both files and each must remain
# effective on its own on a partially deployed box.
#
# What is lost: sudo's PAM session open/close journal lines for this
# user's non-interactive sudo, and any resource limits a session module
# would have applied. Nothing else — the auth and account PAM phases still
# run, sudo still logs the commands themselves, and none of the binaries
# granted below need a login session. This supersedes the fragile
# XDG_SESSION_CLASS=background unit workaround (commit 20956a4, still
# tripping denials on live boxes); those Environment= lines are kept as
# belt-and-braces and pinned by tests/test_service_user.py. See
# AGENT_CONTEXT.md "Status right now".
Defaults:@SERVICE_USER@ !pam_session
@SERVICE_USER@ ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/usermod ^-L [A-Za-z0-9_.-]+$, /usr/bin/usermod ^-L [A-Za-z0-9_.-]+$, /usr/sbin/usermod ^-U [A-Za-z0-9_.-]+$, /usr/bin/usermod ^-U [A-Za-z0-9_.-]+$
@SERVICE_USER@ ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/loginctl ^terminate-user [A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_.-]*$, /usr/bin/loginctl ^terminate-user [A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_.-]*$
@SERVICE_USER@ ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/loginctl ^show-user [A-Za-z0-9_.-]+ -p Sessions --value$, /usr/bin/loginctl ^show-user [A-Za-z0-9_.-]+ -p Sessions --value$
@SERVICE_USER@ ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/loginctl ^lock-session [A-Za-z0-9_.-]+( [A-Za-z0-9_.-]+)*$, /usr/bin/loginctl ^lock-session [A-Za-z0-9_.-]+( [A-Za-z0-9_.-]+)*$
@SERVICE_USER@ ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/loginctl ^show-session [A-Za-z0-9_.-]+ -p Class -p Type$, /usr/bin/loginctl ^show-session [A-Za-z0-9_.-]+ -p Class -p Type$
@SERVICE_USER@ ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/passwd ^-S [A-Za-z0-9_.-]+$, /usr/bin/passwd ^-S [A-Za-z0-9_.-]+$
@SERVICE_USER@ ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/systemd-run ^--machine=[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+@[.]host --user notify-send -u critical .* .*$, /usr/bin/systemd-run ^--machine=[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+@[.]host --user notify-send -u critical .* .*$
# Session-agent feed drain (SESSION_AGENT_PLAN.md §8.1): the panel pulls one
# child's focus feed on the budget tick. sudo's runas is root because the
# systemd machine transport needs it (see the notify note above), but the
# payload runs inside the CHILD's own user manager, so the grant gives the
# service user nothing the child could not already read. The argv is pinned:
# exact flags, `cat`, and an exact /run/user/<uid>/parental-controls/focus.jsonl
# path shape — no other command, path or machine is authorized.
@SERVICE_USER@ ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/systemd-run ^--machine=[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+@[.]host --user --wait --collect cat /run/user/[0-9]+/parental-controls/focus[.]jsonl$, /usr/bin/systemd-run ^--machine=[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+@[.]host --user --wait --collect cat /run/user/[0-9]+/parental-controls/focus[.]jsonl$
